---
name: tack
description: Plan and inspect switches of a stateful workload's primary between sites with the tack CLI, and read SiteFailover ledgers. Use when asked about a Tack run, a site switchover or failover, or a SiteFailover. Never start or resume a run without an explicit instruction from a human for that run.
---

# tack

Switch a stateful workload's primary between sites, with GitOps kept in step.

```text
Tack switches a stateful workload's primary from one site to another: a
planned switchover, or a failover after a site is lost. It acts through the
workload's own API, keeps GitOps in step, and records every step in a
SiteFailover ledger in the new primary's cluster.

A run takes four phases, each step recorded before it starts and after it ends:

  Hold             annotate only the objects the run changes with
                   kustomize.toolkit.fluxcd.io/reconcile: disabled, so Flux
                   stops applying them
  Failover         change the live objects through the workload's API, in
                   order, waiting for each condition
  PromoteToGitOps  commit to Git the values that are now live
  Release          check that Git equals live, remove the annotations, and
                   check that Flux changed nothing

The operator starts every run. Tack never decides to fail over by itself.

Rules for AI agents:
  - An agent never starts "tack run" or "tack resume" without an explicit
    instruction from a human for that run.
  - "tack plan" comes first. Show its output to the human before any run.
  - An agent never passes --yes, --fence, --accept-git-head or --take-over
    unless the human's instruction for that run names it.
  - "tack plan" and "tack status" only read; an agent may run them freely.
  - When a run stops, report its reason and message to the human and wait.

Exit codes:
  0  success
  1  error
  2  usage error
  3  the run stopped and waits for the operator (see tack status)
  4  the run waits for something outside it, such as Git (tack resume later)
```

## Commands

### tack plan

Print every step and change of a run, and change nothing.

```text
Plan prints every step and every change a run would make, in order, and
changes nothing. It reads each reachable site and the Git repository.

The plan names the objects Tack holds, the patches and waits of the
workload's driver, the exact Git change (file, path and value), the checks
before and after release, and the follow-ups left for the operator. Values
captured during the run, such as a demotion token, appear as $(name).

Run "tack plan" before every "tack run", and show its output to the human
who decides.
```

Usage: `tack plan -f WORKLOAD --to SITE [--mode planned|site-loss] [flags]`

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `-f`, `--file` | the Workload file (required) |
| `--from` | the site of the current primary (default: the primary the target site declares) |
| `--mode` | planned: the old primary demotes and hands over its token; site-loss: promote without a token after the old site is lost (default `planned`) |
| `-o`, `--output` | text, or yaml for the SiteFailover the run would create (default `text`) |
| `--to` | the site that becomes primary (required) |

Examples:

```sh
  # A planned switchover to site b
  tack plan -f workload.yaml --to b

  # A failover after site a is lost
  tack plan -f workload.yaml --to b --mode site-loss

  # The SiteFailover object the run would create
  tack plan -f workload.yaml --to b -o yaml
```

### tack resume

Continue a run from its ledger.

```text
Resume continues a run from its ledger, never from memory: it takes the
remaining steps of the plan the run recorded when it started, with the
workload configuration it recorded then.

A step that was started but not recorded as done runs again; every step is
safe to repeat. Steps deferred because their site was unreachable run once
the site is back.

When the run stopped, the ledger's reason says what the operator must
decide. Each decision has its own flag:

  GitChanged           Git moved during the run. Review the new commits, then
                       --accept-git-head SHA makes that commit the new base.
  FencedSiteIsPrimary  a lost site came back and answers as primary.
                       --fence SITE lets the run demote it.
  HeldByAnotherRun     another run holds an object. Finish that run, or
                       start a new run with --take-over.

An agent never starts "tack resume" without an explicit instruction from a
human for that run, and never passes these flags unless that instruction
names them.
```

Usage: `tack resume RUN (-f WORKLOAD | --context CONTEXT) [flags]`

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--accept-git-head` | accept this commit, pushed during the run, as the run's new Git base |
| `--context` | a kube context to read runs from, instead of --file |
| `--fence` | demote this site although it answers as primary (repeatable) |
| `-f`, `--file` | the Workload file: search every site of the workload for its runs |
| `--namespace` | the ledger namespace, with --context (default `tack-system`) |
| `--yes` | skip the confirmation; only when a human approved resuming this run |

Examples:

```sh
  # Continue after Git is back (break-glass)
  tack resume pg-main-20261027-101500 -f workload.yaml

  # Accept a commit pushed during the run, after reviewing it
  tack resume pg-main-20261027-101500 -f workload.yaml --accept-git-head 1a2b3c4

  # Demote a lost site that came back as primary
  tack resume pg-main-20261027-101500 -f workload.yaml --fence a
```

### tack run

Start a run: hold Flux, switch the primary, commit to Git, release.

```text
Run starts a run: it prints the plan, asks for confirmation, creates the
SiteFailover ledger at the target site, and takes the steps in order.

Modes:
  planned    the old primary demotes; its demotion token promotes the new one
  site-loss  the new site promotes without a token; the old primary then
             needs a rebuild. Use it only when the old site is lost.

If Git cannot be reached (break-glass), the run still holds and switches the
live objects, then waits with exit code 4; "tack resume" finishes it once Git
is back. If anything unexpected happens, the run stops with exit code 3 and
the ledger says why.

An agent never starts "tack run" without an explicit instruction from a
human for that run, and runs "tack plan" first.
```

Usage: `tack run -f WORKLOAD --to SITE [--mode planned|site-loss] [flags]`

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `-f`, `--file` | the Workload file (required) |
| `--from` | the site of the current primary (default: the primary the target site declares) |
| `--mode` | planned: the old primary demotes and hands over its token; site-loss: promote without a token after the old site is lost (default `planned`) |
| `--take-over` | take over the holds of this earlier run, which can no longer finish |
| `--to` | the site that becomes primary (required) |
| `--yes` | skip the confirmation; only when a human approved this run |

Examples:

```sh
  # A planned switchover to site b, after reviewing "tack plan"
  tack run -f workload.yaml --to b

  # A failover after site a is lost
  tack run -f workload.yaml --to b --mode site-loss
```

### tack status

Show runs and their steps from the ledger.

```text
Status shows runs from their ledgers. Without RUN it lists the runs of the
workload at every reachable site. With RUN it shows that run's phases, steps,
held objects, Git commits, captured values (by name only) and follow-ups.
```

Usage: `tack status [RUN] (-f WORKLOAD | --context CONTEXT) [flags]`

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--context` | a kube context to read runs from, instead of --file |
| `-f`, `--file` | the Workload file: search every site of the workload for its runs |
| `--namespace` | the ledger namespace, with --context (default `tack-system`) |
| `-o`, `--output` | text or yaml (default `text`) |

Examples:

```sh
  # Every run of the workload
  tack status -f workload.yaml

  # One run, step by step
  tack status pg-main-20261027-101500 -f workload.yaml
```

## Global flags

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--kubeconfig` | kubeconfig file holding every site's context (default: the standard kubeconfig loading rules) |
